// entities.def -- entity definition DSL.
//
// Parsed by src/tools/def_gen.cpp, which emits generated/entities_generated.*
//
// `mesh_asset` and `texture_asset` are asset CLASSES, and they are declared
// nowhere: asset_pack walks resources/ and writes generated/assets.manifest,
// which def_gen reads through --asset-manifest. There is nothing to import and
// nothing to edit -- adding a mesh means dropping a .obj or .mesh into a
// resource directory. An asset-typed field here is spelled assets::mesh_asset
// in the generated structs, because the asset artifacts land in namespace
// `assets` beside the asset system that resolves the ids.
//
// Transcribed 2026-07-26 from the macro declarations in this folder; flags
// audited field by field 2026-07-27 (P4). See entity_def.md at the repo root
// for the design, todo.md for the phase order.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DELIBERATELY ABSENT: the geometry types
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// AABB_Entity, Wedge_Entity, Static_Mesh_Entity and Displacement_Entity are
// NOT here. Per entity_def.md they leave the entity system entirely and become
// plain map-owned C++ value types with handwritten map I/O -- they are never
// networked, so they were paying the blittable/fixed-size constraints for
// nothing. Displacement's [3267]f32 height array is the only reason the DSL
// would ever need fixed-capacity arrays; with geometry gone, it doesn't.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THE THREE FLAGS MEAN  (P4, 2026-07-27 -- these are now real)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// In the macro system only @Editable was ever enforced, so @Networked and
// @Saveable could sit on a field and mean nothing. The generator makes all
// three load-bearing. Every field below has been decided, not inherited:
//
//   @Networked  The server replicates this field to clients in snapshots.
//               Earn it: the client must READ the field AND have no other way
//               to get it. A map-placed entity's static config is already on
//               the client -- it loaded the same map -- so replicating it is
//               bandwidth spent to tell the client what it already knows.
//
//   @Editable   The editor inspector shows and edits it. The inspector only
//               ever walks map entities, so this is unreachable on a
//               @runtime_only type -- and the generator now rejects it there.
//
//   @Saveable   Written to and read from the .source map file. Same
//               reachability rule as @Editable, same generator check.
//
// Two structural rules the generator enforces, both added in P4:
//   * A component-typed field carries NO flags. The flags on the component's
//     own fields are what every consumer reads; a flag at the use site would
//     be silently ignored. (`volume: Box_Volume @Editable` used to do exactly
//     that, sitting next to four unflagged component fields that meant the
//     same thing.)
//   * @Editable / @Saveable on a @runtime_only entity is an error, not a
//     no-op. The base's fields are exempt by construction: `Entity` is not
//     itself @runtime_only, and its flags describe the map-placed types.
//
// A component-typed field carries no flags, but it CAN carry a default, written
// as a literal naming the fields it differs on:
//
//     render: Render = { mesh = .Leet_Full }
//
// A field the literal does not name keeps the component's own default, so the
// component stays the one place a neutral value is written and a use site says
// only what is special about it. Literals nest (`{ material = { color = ... } }`)
// and their order does not matter -- the generator sorts them.
//
// This exists so a per-type value has ONE home. Every one of these used to be a
// hand-written assignment in a spawn function, and they had already drifted:
// bot-fired rockets lived 5 seconds while player-fired ones lived 20, and a
// trigger volume was born {1,1,1} everywhere except the editor's placement tool,
// which said {64,64,64}.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ORIENTATION  (euler until 2026-08-31; a quaternion since)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CANONICAL: `orientation` is a unit QUATERNION, stored x y z w -- the same
// order `linalg::quatf` and the `.animation` channel lines already use.
//
// It was Euler XYZ in degrees, and the reason it stopped being that is not
// interpolation. Euler ADDITION IS NOT ROTATION COMPOSITION: with Rz*Ry*Rx, a
// delta added into .z is a world-frame turn, one added into .x is a local-frame
// turn, and one added into .y is neither -- three rings of one gizmo, three
// different meanings, one of them undefined. Every authored rotation in the map
// was composed that way. rotation_def.md has the four sites and the argument.
//
// Construct one, never spell one: `from_axis_angle` for a ring drag,
// `from_view_angles` for anything derived from an aim, `from_euler_degrees`
// ONLY in the file reader's legacy arm. `to_euler_degrees` seeds the inspector
// widget and has no other caller -- a decomposition is not unique, so a second
// one would fight the first.
//
// NOT the same thing as heading, and this is the line that did NOT move. A
// Player's facing is view_angle_yaw / view_angle_pitch, and those stay two
// floats: an aim is 2-DOF with a clamp and a wrap, both defined on the angles,
// and roll is not merely unused but unrepresentable -- which is a property
// worth keeping in the type. `orientation` on a Player is written once at spawn
// and is then vestigial. rotation_def.md §2 and §5 argue it, including against
// the forward-vector spelling.


Fully_Serializable :: [@Networked, @Editable, @Saveable]

Entity :: base {
    entity_id:   u32                    @Networked
    position:    v3                     @Fully_Serializable
    orientation: quat = {0, 0, 0, 1}    @Fully_Serializable
}

Spawn_Type :: enum { Human, Bot }
Team_Allegiance :: enum {Red, Blu, Free_For_All}

Weapon :: enum {Knife, Scout, Rocket_Launcher, Dash}

// HOW a trigger pull resolves.
Fire_Resolution :: enum { Hitscan, Projectile, Self_Impulse }

Inventory_Slot :: enum { Primary, Secondary, Melee, Utility_1, Utility_2 }

Shader_Type :: enum { Lit, Unlit }
Shape_Kind :: enum { Sphere, Box }

Trigger_Action :: enum { Kill, Set_Health, Print_Message, Warp_To_Spawn, Complete_Level, Checkpoint, Grant_Weapon, Set_Velocity, Give_Impulse}
Fire_Mode :: enum { On_Enter, Every_Tick }
Aim_Pose :: enum { Forward, Upward, Downward, Left, Right }

Box_Volume :: component {
    position: v3 = {0, 0, 0}            @Editable @Saveable
    half_extents: v3 = {1, 1, 1}        @Editable @Saveable
}

Material :: component {
    shader_type: Shader_Type = .Lit     @Fully_Serializable
    color:       v3 = {1, 1, 1}         @Fully_Serializable
    roughness:   f32 = 0.5              @Fully_Serializable
}

Render :: component {
    mesh:         mesh_asset = .Missing @Fully_Serializable
    visible:      bool = true           @Fully_Serializable
    is_wireframe: bool = false          @Fully_Serializable
    offset:       v3 = {0, 0, 0}        @Fully_Serializable
    scale:        v3 = {1, 1, 1}        @Fully_Serializable
    rotation:     quat = {0, 0, 0, 1}   @Fully_Serializable
    material:     Material
}

// WHERE a light is evaluated, and it is a correctness requirement rather than a
// tuning knob: the bake walks every light in the map, so the day a runtime light
// array exists with no mode, every static light is counted TWICE -- once out of
// the atlas and once analytically. lighting_def.md ss2.
//
// Mixed is the middle value it is tempting to skip, and it is what makes a
// character look like they are in the room. A lightmap lives on brush face
// charts, so a player receives NOTHING from a baked light -- they are not in the
// atlas. With only Baked and Dynamic an author chooses between a level light
// that ignores characters and one that costs a runtime slot and casts no shadow.
Light_Mode :: enum { Baked, Mixed, Dynamic }

Light :: component {
    color:     v3 = {1, 1, 1}   @Editable @Saveable
    intensity: f32 = 1.0        @Editable @Saveable
    // Baked is the default because level fill light is most of them, and because
    // it is what every light already in a .source file was when it was placed.
    mode:      Light_Mode = .Baked  @Editable @Saveable
    // The emitter's RADIUS, in engine units, and the name is deliberately not
    // `range`: range is how far the light REACHES, this is how big the thing
    // emitting it is, and they are unrelated quantities that want the same word.
    // Unreal ships SourceRadius beside AttenuationRadius for the same reason.
    //
    // Zero is a punctual light and is exactly the shading and the bake this had
    // before area lights existed, which is why every .source file on disk needs
    // no conversion. Above zero it buys three things that have to move together
    // or the surface carries two lighting models: a penumbra in the bake, a
    // broadened specular highlight at runtime, and a near-field falloff clamp
    // (a true inverse square diverges at zero distance for anything with size).
    //
    // A DIRECTIONAL light ignores it -- the sun is infinitely far away, so its
    // softness is an angle rather than a length, and it carries its own
    // angular_diameter_degrees instead.
    source_radius: f32 = 0.0    @Editable @Saveable
}

Movement :: component {
    air_jumps_used: u8                  @Networked
    is_grounded: bool                   @Networked
    time_since_grounded_seconds: f32    @Networked
    jump_was_held: bool                 @Networked
    seconds_until_impulse_ready: f32    @Networked
}


Inventory :: component {
    weapons:              u32[Inventory_Slot]  @Networked
    active_slot:          Inventory_Slot = .Melee  @Networked
    deploy_complete_time: u64
}

// Room to grow, if the need shows up -- same slot, same syntax:
//   @no_network   nothing about this type is ever replicated; makes @Networked
//                 on any of its fields an error. Trigger_Volume and
//                 Player_Spawn would both take it today.
//   @singleton    at most one per session (a gamemode / world-settings entity).
//   @category("Lighting")  grouping for the placement menu -- the first case
//                 that wants an ARGUMENT, which the grammar should be able to
//                 grow without moving the attribute somewhere else.

// Placed in the editor; the server consumes it at load and removes it.
// Nothing about a spawn point survives into a snapshot, so nothing is
// @Networked -- the client never sees one.
Player_Spawn_Entity :: entity {
    spawn_type: Spawn_Type = .Human     @Editable @Saveable
    team_allegiance: Team_Allegiance = .Free_For_All @Editable @Saveable 
}

// this does not need anything, really.
Player_Spectate_Entity:: entity {
}


Player_Entity :: entity @runtime_only {
    // The remote player's model is oriented from these, so they replicate.
    view_angle_yaw:    f32              @Networked
    view_angle_pitch:  f32              @Networked
    body_yaw:          f32              @Networked

    health:            i32              @Networked

    death_tick:        u32              @Networked
    last_fire_tick:    u32              @Networked
    last_fire_weapon:  Weapon = .Knife  @Networked

    // WHEN the in-flight reload finishes, in sub-tick time; 0 means not
    // reloading. A DEADLINE rather than a start stamp, because the duration is
    // a property of the weapon being reloaded and the weapon can change while
    // the reload runs -- at a sub-tick moment inside the same step loop, now
    // that the weapon keys carry edges. Storing the start would leave "whose
    // duration applies" to be re-decided every tick against a weapon that is no
    // longer the one being reloaded; storing the deadline settles it at the
    // moment the input arrived, which is the only moment that knows.
    reload_complete_time: u64

    // Rate limit for the empty-magazine warning. Server-only, like the two
    // above: a log cadence is not something a client has any use for.
    last_empty_fire_warning_tick: u32

    // uid of the Trigger_Action::Checkpoint volume last touched; 0 for none.
    checkpoint_uid: u32

    last_hit_tick:         u32          @Networked
    last_hit_was_headshot: bool         @Networked


    // How a client works out which player entity is itself.
    client_slot_index: i32              @Networked

    // Display name, sanitized from CmdConnect at the connect site. On the
    // ENTITY rather than in a message of its own so a late joiner gets every
    // name in its first full snapshot, with no backlog to replay -- and so a
    // bot, which has no client, still has a name.
    name:   string<32>                  @Networked

    // The scoreboard, and the only score the wire carries: team score changes
    // once per phase and rides Round_Phase_Changed instead. Awarded at the
    // one health crossing in damage.cpp.
    kills:  i32                         @Networked
    deaths: i32                         @Networked
    // Feeds remote-player interpolation.
    velocity:          v3               @Networked

    inventory:         Inventory

    // Per-player movement state. Sits beside velocity rather than inside it
    // because velocity is what the world does to you and this is what you may
    // still do to the world.
    movement:          Movement

    render:            Render = { mesh = .Leet_Full }
    team_allegiance: Team_Allegiance = .Free_For_All @Networked
}

//@NOTE(SJM): this is a hacky test to embed in weapon_entity, but it's a test.
Damage_Type :: enum {Normal, Orange, Teal}


Weapon_Entity :: entity {
    ammo:             i32               @Networked
    weapon_id:        Weapon            @Networked
    owner_uid:        u32
    next_fire_time:   u64
    damage_type: Damage_Type = .Normal  @Editable @Saveable
    render:           Render
}

Rocket_Entity :: entity @runtime_only {
    velocity:        v3                 @Networked
    lifetime: f32 = 5.0
    damage_amount: f32 = 50.0
    damage_radius: f32 = 120.0
    knockback_force: f32 = 600.0
    owner_id:        u32
    collision_radius: f32 = 12.0        @Networked
    render:          Render
}

Particle_Emitter_Entity :: entity {
    sprite:             texture_asset = .Smoke @Editable @Saveable
    emit_rate:          f32 = 20.0            @Editable @Saveable
    max_particles:      i32 = 64              @Editable @Saveable
    lifetime_min:       f32 = 0.5             @Editable @Saveable
    lifetime_max:       f32 = 1.5             @Editable @Saveable
    velocity_min:       f32 = 2.0             @Editable @Saveable
    velocity_max:       f32 = 5.0             @Editable @Saveable
    spread:             f32 = 0.5             @Editable @Saveable
    gravity:            v3 = {0, 0.5, 0}      @Editable @Saveable
    drag:               f32 = 0.3             @Editable @Saveable
    size_start:         f32 = 0.5             @Editable @Saveable
    size_end:           f32 = 2.0             @Editable @Saveable
    rotation_speed_min: f32 = -1.0            @Editable @Saveable
    rotation_speed_max: f32 = 1.0             @Editable @Saveable
    color_start:        v3 = {1, 1, 1}        @Editable @Saveable
    color_end:          v3 = {0.5, 0.5, 0.5}  @Editable @Saveable
    alpha_start:        f32 = 0.8             @Editable @Saveable
    alpha_end:          f32 = 0.0             @Editable @Saveable
    emitter_lifetime:   f32 = 0.0             @Editable @Saveable
    parent_entity_id:   u32 = 0
}

Damageable_Entity :: entity {
    max_health:        i32 = 100                @Editable @Saveable
    health:            i32 = 100                @Networked
    hitbox_half_extents: v3 = {16, 32, 16}      @Editable @Saveable
    weakness: Damage_Type = .Orange              @Editable @Saveable
    render:            Render
}

Trigger_Volume_Entity :: entity {
    // 64 is the editor's grid-sized starting box -- big enough to see and to
    // drag. Box_Volume's own {1,1,1} stays neutral for whatever embeds it next.
    volume:            Box_Volume = { half_extents = {64, 64, 64} }
    action:            Trigger_Action = .Kill   @Editable @Saveable
    fire_mode:         Fire_Mode = .On_Enter    @Editable @Saveable
    // 64 is enough for a spawn name to match against. param_string is
    // player-facing message text (Print_Message), so it gets room for a real
    // sentence -- 250 was never a chosen number, just the pascal_string_t
    // default every field inherited. Neither is networked, so the size costs
    // struct memory and map file bytes, not bandwidth. 255 is the hard ceiling.
    param_target_name: string<64>               @Editable @Saveable
    param_string:      string<128>              @Editable @Saveable
    param_float:       f32 = 0.0                @Editable @Saveable
}

// THREE TYPES, NOT ONE WITH A KIND ENUM. This was `Light_Entity` plus a
// Light_Type of {Point, Spot, Directional}, and of its seven fields only color
// and intensity were meaningful for all three: `range` is dead for
// directional, `direction` for point, and both cone angles for everything but
// spot. An enum that selects WHICH FIELDS ARE LIVE is a type; an enum that
// selects a behavior over fields that are all live is an enum, which is what
// Shape_Kind above still correctly is.
//
// The split is authoring-side ONLY. A GPU light array is homogeneous, so the
// shader keeps one struct with a type tag in it (pbr.frag reads that tag out of
// spot_params.w) and a gather pass turns all three of these into it. Plural at
// authoring time, singular at the GPU, and conflating the two is what makes the
// enum look mandatory when it is not.
//
// What it buys, and the reason it was worth doing: the editor's five per-type
// switches each draw the RIGHT helper -- the falloff sphere, the cone, the
// direction arrow -- instead of one yellow cross plus an inner switch on `kind`
// at every one of them.
//
// `direction` is gone with the enum, and not because it was per-kind: base
// Entity already carries `orientation`, so it was a second copy of the same
// fact. Spot and directional read their facing off that, which is what makes
// the rotate gizmo work on a light. Point lights have no facing to be wrong
// about, which is precisely the ambiguity that kept the duplicate alive.
//
// None of these is @Networked, and that is not an oversight: a light is placed
// in the editor and read back out of the MAP, and the client loads the same
// map, so replicating it is bandwidth spent telling the client what it already
// knows. When dynamic lights (muzzle flash, explosion) arrive they are
// runtime-spawned, and THAT is the change that earns @Networked back.

Point_Light_Entity :: entity {
    light: Light
    // Where the falloff reaches zero, in engine units -- 1 unit = 1 inch, the
    // scale player_constants.hpp's 32x32x72 hull fixes. So 256 is a 21-foot
    // (6.5m) radius: one room, two editor grid cells. Between Godot's 5m
    // OmniLight3D default and Unreal's 10m PointLight one, and deliberately
    // smaller than the spot below, which is a directed beam rather than a bulb.
    //
    // Do NOT read the scale off g_gravity: 800 is arcade gravity, ~2x real, and
    // the only constant here that does not respect the inch mapping.
    range: f32 = 256.0                  @Editable @Saveable
}

Spot_Light_Entity :: entity {
    light:         Light
    // 512 units = 42 feet (13m): a beam down a corridor rather than a room.
    range:         f32 = 512.0          @Editable @Saveable
    // HALF-angle in degrees, measured from the cone axis -- which is what
    // pbr.frag's `dot(-L, direction)` compares against, and what Unreal and
    // Godot both mean by a spot angle. Inner is where falloff starts, outer
    // where it reaches zero; inner > outer inverts the gradient rather than
    // being rejected, and the editor cone draws what the value says.
    inner_degrees: f32 = 20.0           @Editable @Saveable
    outer_degrees: f32 = 35.0           @Editable @Saveable
}

// No range and no position falloff -- a directional light is a direction and a
// colour. It still has a `position`, inherited from the base, and the editor
// draws its gizmo there; nothing shading reads it.
Directional_Light_Entity :: entity {
    light: Light
    // The FULL angle the disc of the emitter subtends, in degrees -- the sun is
    // 0.53 in reality, and an overcast sky is effectively the whole hemisphere.
    // A directional light has no position for a radius to be measured from, so
    // this is what Light::source_radius is for every other kind: the same
    // geometry with the distance divided out.
    //
    // Zero is the hard parallel sun this had before, so no map converts and a
    // bake pays for extra shadow rays only where an author asked for softness.
    angular_diameter_degrees: f32 = 0.0     @Editable @Saveable
}

// Both map-placed AND runtime-spawned (the spawn_cube / spawn_sphere console
// commands), which is why its Render component has to be @Networked while its
// own configuration does not: play_state.cpp draws a body from
// body.position / body.orientation / body.render and reads nothing else off it.
Physics_Body_Entity :: entity {
    // Jolt's shape and the editor's size box. Server-side simulation input;
    // the visual size rides in render.scale, which the spawn path sets from
    // the same extents.
    shape:    Shape_Kind = .Box         @Editable @Saveable
    size:     v3                        @Editable @Saveable
    // Kept @Networked for the interpolation item in todo.md: bodies currently
    // snap to each snapshot, and velocity is what a lerp/extrapolate needs.
    velocity: v3                        @Networked
    mass:     f32 = 10.0                @Editable @Saveable
    render:   Render
}
